+---
+node: record-store
+---
+
+# Decision: Whole-visit revisions as the record store's concurrency mechanism
+
+## Context
+
+Twenty-four field workers share twelve Android tablets and survey 320 permanent plots across a season of roughly 6,000 plot visits. Tablets are offline for up to 48 hours, and workers sometimes correct the same visit independently after comparing field notes, so two submissions for one visit can arrive days apart, each written from a state the other never saw. Today a spreadsheet import overwrites the later correction without showing which submission was lost.
+
+The record store therefore has to answer a question with scientific weight: when two workers have edited the same visit, what does the visit say, and how is that answer reached? The answer is not only a storage question. The exported visit is the record that reaches the regional science partner, so the mechanism decides whether a coordinator can see that a species identification and its cover estimate were changed together, whether the exported value can be traced back to the exact submission that produced it, and whether a wrong combination can be noticed before it is exported.
+
+Two mechanisms are credible. Whole-visit revisions keep every submitted visit intact and let a coordinator choose between competing complete revisions. Field-aware three-way merge combines edits to unrelated fields automatically and sends only overlapping edits to review. The team chose whole-visit revisions, accepting a larger review queue in exchange for never having the server combine edits that are scientifically related to each other without a person deciding that combination is right.
+
+This decision owns the record store's submission envelope, its notion of a current visit, and the representation of unresolved work. It obligates the tablet-sync and review-export nodes; those obligations are listed in Selection.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+### Gates
+
+- **A change to a scientific field reaches the exported record only through a whole visit that a worker submitted or a coordinator chose.** No mechanism may assemble a visit from pieces of two submissions without a person deciding that the assembled visit is scientifically right. (Doc-local gate.)
+- **No submitted edit is discarded.** Both accepted submissions survive whatever the mechanism does with them. (Inherited from the tenet "Do not silently discard a submitted edit".)
+
+### Weighted criteria
+
+- **A wrong combination of edits is detectable before export.** How likely is it that the mechanism produces a visit combining edits that should have been judged together, and can a person notice that before the visit reaches the partner? *Major.* (Turns on the tenet "Keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable".)
+- **The exported value can be traced to the submission that produced it.** How directly can a coordinator or the science partner connect an exported field back to the worker's submitted visit and any decision applied to it? *Major.* (Turns on the same inspectability tenet.)
+- **The review workflow stays operable by two coordinators without a systems administrator.** How much standing configuration, rule authoring, and queue triage does the mechanism impose on the people who run it each week? *Major.* (Turns on the tenet "Prefer workflows two coordinators can operate without a dedicated systems administrator".)
+- **Review workload.** How much coordinator attention does a season's worth of corrections consume? *Moderate* — the team has accepted a larger queue in exchange for the criteria above, so this is a cost to be managed rather than a cost to be minimized.
+- **Maintenance burden of the mechanism's rules.** How much ongoing upkeep does the mechanism need as the visit structure evolves — new fields, new list-shaped fields, new relationships between fields? *Moderate.*
+- **Tablet complexity under disconnection.** How much state must a tablet carry across a 48-hour offline window, and how much must it reconcile before a worker can submit? *Minor.*
+- **Submission size on a poor link.** How much data does a submission carry when a tablet finally reconnects? *Minor.*
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Whole-visit revisions
+
+Each submission names the revision it was edited from and carries the complete visit. The server stores every submission as an immutable revision. A submission made against the visit's current revision, when no other submission is pending, becomes the new current revision. Any other submission — one written against an older revision, or one arriving while another submission is already pending — is kept as a candidate, and the current revision stays frozen until a coordinator chooses. The coordinator selects one candidate or dismisses them all; the server never assembles a visit from more than one submission.
+
+*Demonstration.* A visit has current revision R1. Worker A corrects an observer name from R1 and submits it with base R1; it becomes current revision R2. Worker B, offline since before R2, revises a species identification and its cover estimate from R1 and submits with base R1; it becomes a candidate C1. The visit is under review. The coordinator compares R2 and C1 as complete visits, selects one, and the export carries that visit and the trail of the other.
+
+- **Scientific edits judged together**: passes. Each candidate is a complete visit, so a species identification and its cover estimate are judged as one unit by construction.
+- **No submitted edit discarded**: passes. Every submission is retained as a revision.
+- **Detectability of a wrong combination**: strong fit. No combination is ever assembled without a coordinator's decision, so there is no class of silently wrong visit to detect.
+- **Traceability to the submitting visit**: strong fit. The exported visit is one specific revision whose payload is exactly what a worker submitted.
+- **Operability by two coordinators**: partial fit. The review flow itself is simple — compare, choose — but the queue is larger than it would otherwise be, and every visit with any pending correction occupies a coordinator's attention even when the corrections are unrelated.
+- **Review workload**: weak fit. Two workers editing one visit at all produces review work, however unrelated the edits are. Over a season of 6,000 visits with independent corrections, the queue grows with corrections rather than with conflicts.
+- **Maintenance burden**: strong fit. No rules to maintain; new visit fields need no merge semantics.
+- **Tablet complexity**: partial fit. A tablet must track the base revision ID of every local edit and rebase an edit whose base has been superseded, which it learns from the server's response. No base snapshots or change sets are stored.
+- **Submission size**: weak fit. Every submission carries the whole visit, including species lists.
+
+*Summary.* Trades review volume for the guarantee that no visit is assembled by the machine.
+
+### Field-aware three-way merge
+
+Each submission supplies the base state it was edited from plus a structured set of field changes. The server compares the changes against the base and the current state and combines changes that touch different fields. Two submissions that change the same field, or that touch fields the merge rules judge related, are held for review as a conflict.
+
+*Demonstration.* A visit has base B and current C. Worker A changes an observer name; worker B adds a missing species. The changes touch different fields, so the server combines them and the visit advances without review. Worker C revises a species identification while worker D revises that species' cover estimate; the rules judge those fields related, so the server holds both for review instead of combining them.
+
+- **Scientific edits judged together**: partial fit. Related edits are caught only where the merge rules recognize the relationship. A relationship the rules do not model is combined silently, which is precisely the failure the team is unwilling to accept.
+- **No submitted edit discarded**: passes. Both submissions survive, and the combined result keeps the provenance of each contributing field.
+- **Detectability of a wrong combination**: weak fit. The mechanism's normal output is a combined visit, so a wrong combination is only detectable if a person or a rule notices it. Detection depends on the completeness of rules the server cannot write without domain knowledge the project has not encoded.
+- **Traceability to the submitting visit**: partial fit. An exported field traces to a contributing submission, but the exported visit as a whole corresponds to no single submission.
+- **Operability by two coordinators**: weak fit. The rules are standing configuration that someone must author and maintain, and rule quality determines how much review the queue receives. The team has no dedicated systems administrator for that.
+- **Review workload**: strong fit. Only overlapping or related edits reach a coordinator, so the queue tracks actual conflicts rather than all corrections.
+- **Maintenance burden**: weak fit. Every new field, every list-shaped field, and every relationship between fields needs merge semantics written, tested, and maintained. Scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries each need their own handling, and the rules encode assumptions about scientific relationships that the server cannot verify.
+- **Tablet complexity**: partial fit. A tablet must keep the base snapshot it edited from and construct structured changes, and it must reconcile a locally changed base against a superseded current state before it can submit.
+- **Submission size**: strong fit. A submission carries only what changed.
+
+*Summary.* Trades a standing rule-maintenance obligation for a smaller review queue.
+
+## Selection
+
+Whole-visit revisions are the record store's concurrency mechanism. The doc-local gate — that a scientific change reaches the exported record only through a whole visit a person submitted or chose — eliminates field-aware merge outright, because automatic combination is the mechanism's normal output and the gate admits no exceptions for fields the rules judge unrelated. The two options' remaining criteria reinforce the same direction: field-aware merge is weak on detectability of a wrong combination and on traceability, both major and both turning on the inspectability tenet, and it is weak on operability and maintenance because the rules encode scientific relationships the server cannot verify without domain knowledge no one on the team has formalized.
+
+The decision turns on two tenets: "Do not silently discard a submitted edit", which both options satisfy and which makes the difference lie elsewhere, and "Keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable", which whole-visit revisions satisfy in the strongest available form — the exported visit is one worker's submitted visit, and every competing visit remains visible beside it.
+
+### What this forecloses
+
+- The server never combines fields from two submissions. A coordinator selects one complete revision or dismisses all candidates; no resolution authors a merged revision.
+- No review question is asked of a field-level diff. A candidate is always a complete visit.
+- The record store holds no field-level provenance structure, because no exported visit is a composite.
+
+### What this obligates downstream
+
+**Record store.** Accepts a submission envelope carrying the visit ID, the base revision ID, the complete visit payload, and an idempotency key. Stores every submission as an immutable revision with its author and receipt time. Distinguishes a visit's current revision from its pending candidates and reports the visit's state. Exposes review work as a list of visits under review with their candidates, and a resolution operation that records the coordinator, a disposition per candidate, and the resulting current revision. Exposes visit state as the signal for export eligibility.
+
+**Tablet-sync.** Carries the base revision ID of every local edit alongside the local visit, and sends the complete visit with each submission rather than a change set. On every server response it learns the visit's current revision ID and state, and on learning that its base revision is no longer current it either rebases the pending edit onto the current revision or tells the worker that the visit is under review. It mints and stores an idempotency key per intended edit, kept across retries. It does not hold base snapshots of superseded revisions and does not construct structured changes. Because each submission is a whole visit, a tablet that loses connectivity mid-submission re-sends a payload it already holds rather than reconstructing one.
+
+**Review-export.** Presents competing whole visits side by side rather than a field-level diff, so the coordinator sees the whole of each submitted observation while choosing. Records one disposition per candidate — selected or dismissed — and submits them together as a single resolution. Holds visits that are under review out of the export set, and exports the current revision of resolved visits only, so an exported column traces to a specific submitted visit. Carries the larger queue this decision creates: every visit with any pending correction reaches a coordinator, including corrections to unrelated fields.
+
+### When the other approach would be reconsidered
+
+Field-aware merge remains the credible alternative, and the case for it is real rather than a strawman. It wins on review workload, which matters more than this decision allows: if the queue grows past what two coordinators can work through before the Friday export, the mechanism is failing the team on its own terms. It wins on tablet complexity and submission size, which matter more if field devices become more constrained than the current generation. And it is the right answer for a workflow where independent corrections to one visit are the common case rather than the exception.
+
+Reconsidering it means changing the gate, and the gate is the decision. A path back exists if the project is willing to accept automatic combination in exchange for the smaller queue, and it becomes available if the scientific relationships between fields are formalized explicitly and maintained by someone whose job it is — at which point the rules would encode known relationships rather than guesses, and the wrong-combination risk would be a matter of rule coverage rather than of principle. Until that formalization exists and the queue is a demonstrated burden, whole-visit revisions hold the line.
