+Terms used below: a **visit** is identified by a client-generated UUID; a **revision** is an immutable stored submission of a complete visit; the **base revision** of a submission is the revision it was edited from; a **candidate revision** is a revision in state `pending-review` awaiting a coordinator's decision; a **closed** revision is one that was current or a candidate and was not selected; a visit's **`visit_state`** is `resolved` when the visit has no candidate revisions and `under-review` while it has one or more; a revision's **`state`** is `current`, `pending-review`, or `closed`; an **idempotency key** is a client-minted key scoped to the submitting author that identifies one intended edit across retries.
+
+## Submission and Idempotency
+
+- [ ] **REC-001**: When an authenticated worker submits a valid visit revision, the system SHALL retain the submitted payload as an immutable revision carrying its visit ID, revision ID, base revision ID, author ID, server receipt time, idempotency key, and revision state.
+- [ ] **REC-002**: When a submission repeats an idempotency key previously accepted from the same author for the same visit, the same base revision, and an identical payload, the system SHALL return the original revision's revision ID without storing a second revision.
+- [ ] **REC-003**: When a submission repeats an idempotency key previously accepted from the same author with a different visit, a different base revision, or a different payload, the system SHALL reject the submission with reason `idempotency_conflict` and leave the original submission unchanged.
+- [ ] **REC-004**: When edits derived from the same starting visit state are accepted, the system SHALL retain both original submissions as revisions regardless of the concurrency policy's outcome.
+- [ ] **REC-007**: The system SHALL scope idempotency keys to the submitting author, so that the same key presented by a different author is treated as a distinct submission.
+- [ ] **REC-008**: When a submission is rejected, the system SHALL store no revision, SHALL consume no idempotency key, and SHALL return a machine-readable rejection reason indicating whether retrying may succeed.
+- [ ] **REC-009**: When a submission fails validation, the system SHALL report every failing field in a single response.
+- [ ] **REC-010**: When a submission is accepted, the system SHALL return the stored revision's revision ID, that revision's state as of acceptance, the visit's `visit_state`, and the visit's current revision ID.
+- [ ] **REC-011**: When a caller submits a visit revision without authenticating as a worker, the system SHALL reject the submission.
+- [ ] **REC-012**: The system SHALL validate the entire submission envelope before storing any part of it.
+
+## Validation
+
+- [ ] **REC-013**: When a submission names a visit ID that is not a UUID, the system SHALL reject the submission with reason `invalid_envelope`.
+- [ ] **REC-014**: When a submission names a plot that does not resolve in the site catalog, the system SHALL reject the submission with reason `invalid_envelope`.
+- [ ] **REC-015**: When a submission's payload contains a species entry whose cover estimate falls outside the range 0 to 100, the system SHALL reject the submission with reason `invalid_envelope`.
+- [ ] **REC-016**: When a submission's payload contains two species entries with the same species name, the system SHALL reject the submission with reason `invalid_envelope`.
+- [ ] **REC-017**: When a submission's payload contains two observer entries with the same name, the system SHALL reject the submission with reason `invalid_envelope`.
+- [ ] **REC-018**: When a submission's payload contains a species entry or an observer entry with an empty name, the system SHALL reject the submission with reason `invalid_envelope`.
+- [ ] **REC-019**: When a submission's payload omits the observation date or gives a value that is not a calendar date, the system SHALL reject the submission with reason `invalid_envelope`.
+- [ ] **REC-020**: When a submission's payload omits the notes field, the system SHALL reject the submission with reason `invalid_envelope`.
+- [ ] **REC-021**: When a submission's payload omits the observer list, the system SHALL reject the submission with reason `invalid_envelope`.
+- [ ] **REC-022**: When a submission's payload omits the species list, the system SHALL reject the submission with reason `invalid_envelope`.
+- [ ] **REC-023**: The system SHALL NOT reject a submission on grounds of scientific plausibility.
+- [ ] **REC-024**: After a submission is accepted, the system SHALL NOT re-check the stored revision's plot reference against the site catalog.
+
+## Concurrency Policy
+
+- [ ] **REC-025**: When the first submission for a visit is accepted, the system SHALL store it as the visit's current revision.
+- [ ] **REC-026**: When a submission that is neither a replay of an accepted submission nor an idempotency conflict names the visit's current revision as its base revision, and the visit has no candidate revisions, the system SHALL store the submission as the visit's new current revision and close the prior current revision.
+- [ ] **REC-027**: When a submission that is neither a replay of an accepted submission nor an idempotency conflict names a closed revision of the same visit as its base revision, the system SHALL store the submission as a candidate revision and set the visit's `visit_state` to `under-review`.
+- [ ] **REC-028**: While a visit has one or more candidate revisions, the system SHALL store any further valid submission for that visit as a candidate revision and SHALL NOT advance the visit's current revision.
+- [ ] **REC-029**: When a submission names a base revision belonging to a different visit, the system SHALL reject the submission with reason `base_belongs_to_other_visit`.
+- [ ] **REC-030**: When a submission names a base revision the system does not hold, the system SHALL reject the submission with reason `unknown_base`, which the system SHALL mark as retryable.
+- [ ] **REC-031**: When a submission names a candidate revision as its base revision, the system SHALL reject the submission with reason `base_is_candidate`.
+- [ ] **REC-032**: When a submission that is neither a replay of an accepted submission nor an idempotency conflict is accepted for a visit that already has a current revision and names no base revision, the system SHALL store the submission as a candidate revision and set the visit's `visit_state` to `under-review`.
+- [ ] **REC-033**: The system SHALL compare a submission's base revision and write the resulting revision as one atomic step, so that of two submissions naming the same base revision exactly one becomes the visit's current revision and the other a candidate revision.
+- [ ] **REC-034**: The system SHALL assign revision IDs from a single monotonic sequence per visit.
+- [ ] **REC-035**: The system SHALL place every revision in exactly one of the states `current`, `pending-review`, and `closed`.
+- [ ] **REC-036**: The system SHALL NOT delete a closed revision and SHALL NOT return a closed revision to a candidate state.
+- [ ] **REC-037**: The system SHALL allow each revision to take part in at most one resolution.
+- [ ] **REC-038**: The system SHALL NOT create a revision whose payload combines fields from more than one candidate revision.
+
+## Retrieval
+
+- [ ] **REC-005**: When an authenticated caller retrieves a visit, the system SHALL indicate whether unresolved review work exists for that visit.
+- [ ] **REC-039**: When an authenticated caller retrieves a visit, the system SHALL return the visit's current revision with its revision ID, base revision ID, author ID, server receipt time, and complete payload.
+- [ ] **REC-040**: When an authenticated caller retrieves a visit that is under review, the system SHALL return the visit's candidate revisions, each with its revision ID, base revision ID, author ID, server receipt time, and complete payload, and SHALL report a candidate with no stated base revision as having no base revision.
+- [ ] **REC-041**: When an authenticated caller retrieves a visit the system does not hold, the system SHALL report the visit as not found.
+- [ ] **REC-042**: When a coordinator or tablet-reconciliation client retrieves the submission history of a visit, the system SHALL return every revision held for that visit in server sequence order, with each revision's state, base revision ID, and any resolution recorded against it.
+- [ ] **REC-043**: When two revisions of a visit share a server receipt time, the system SHALL order them by their server revision sequence.
+- [ ] **REC-044**: When a coordinator lists review work, the system SHALL return the visits that are under review, each with its current revision and its candidate revisions.
+- [ ] **REC-045**: When a submission repeats an idempotency key previously accepted for the same author, visit, base revision, and payload, the system SHALL return the visit's `visit_state` and current revision ID as they stand when the response is produced.
+
+## Review Work and Resolution
+
 - [ ] **REC-006**: When a coordinator resolves review work, the system SHALL retain the coordinator's identity and the contributing submission IDs with the resolution outcome.
+- [ ] **REC-046**: When a coordinator resolves a visit, the system SHALL require a disposition for every candidate revision of that visit, and SHALL reject the resolution when the dispositions omit a candidate revision, name a revision that is not a candidate, or select more than one candidate.
+- [ ] **REC-047**: When a resolution selects a candidate revision, the system SHALL make that revision the visit's current revision, close the prior current revision and every other candidate revision, and set the visit's `visit_state` to `resolved`.
+- [ ] **REC-048**: When a resolution dismisses every candidate revision and selects none, the system SHALL leave the visit's current revision in place and set the visit's `visit_state` to `resolved`.
+- [ ] **REC-049**: When a resolution is submitted and the visit's candidate revisions no longer match the dispositions, the system SHALL reject the resolution.
+- [ ] **REC-050**: When a resolution is applied, the system SHALL record the resolution, its time, and its coordinator identity against each participating candidate revision.
+- [ ] **REC-051**: When a caller submits a resolution without holding the coordinator role, the system SHALL reject the resolution.
+- [ ] **REC-052**: The system SHALL take the coordinator identity of a resolution from the authenticated principal rather than from the request.
+- [ ] **REC-053**: When a resolution is applied, the system SHALL record each candidate revision's disposition, `selected` or `dismissed`, with the resolution.
+
+## Retention and Export Eligibility
+
+- [ ] **REC-054**: The system SHALL retain every revision of a visit, including revisions closed by a resolution.
+- [ ] **REC-055**: When a visit's `visit_state` is `resolved`, the system SHALL report the visit as eligible for export, with its current revision as the settled state.
+- [ ] **REC-056**: While a visit's `visit_state` is `under-review`, the system SHALL report the visit as not eligible for export.
